CHANGE ENTRY #6
Date: 2025-07-31
Topic: Bug Fix — POST /api/ar/reconcile 500 (NaT JSON serialization error)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

OVERVIEW:
  The AR reconcile endpoint returned HTTP 500 with:
    ValueError: NaTType does not support timetuple
  Root cause: clean_date() in ar_column_mapper.py converts date columns to
  pandas Timestamps. When a date cell is blank or unparseable, pandas stores
  NaT (Not a Time). The _df_to_records() helper in ar_reconcile.py used
  df.where(pd.notna(df), None).to_dict() which does NOT convert NaT to None
  — it leaves NaT objects in the dict. Flask's JSON encoder then tried to
  serialize NaT as a datetime via werkzeug's http_date(), which calls
  dt.timetuple() — NaT does not support that, causing the crash.

FILE MODIFIED:

  backend/ar_reconcile.py  [FIXED]
  - BEFORE: _df_to_records used df.where(pd.notna(df), None).to_dict(orient="records")
            which left pandas NaT and Timestamp objects in the output dict.
  - AFTER:  _df_to_records iterates each row and explicitly converts:
              - NaT / NaN / None  → None
              - pd.Timestamp      → ISO 8601 string via .isoformat(), or None if NaT
              - numpy scalar      → Python native via .item()
              - everything else   → passed through as-is
  - IMPACT: All date columns (TxnDate, DueDate) are now safely serialized to
            JSON strings or null. No more 500 on files with blank date cells.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CHANGE ENTRY #7
Date: 2025-07-31
Topic: Bug Fix — POST /api/ar/reconcile 500 (NaT in tier2_rows and src_report)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

OVERVIEW:
  The previous fix (Entry #6) only patched _df_to_records in ar_reconcile.py.
  The 500 persisted because NaT values also appeared in:
    - tier2_rows: built manually via pandas Series.get() from iterrows(),
      which returns raw NaT/Timestamp objects directly.
    - src_report / tgt_report: passed straight from load_and_map() into
      jsonify() without any sanitization.
  Flask's JSON encoder hit NaT in any of these paths and crashed the same way.

FILE MODIFIED:

  backend/app.py  [FIXED]
  - Added _sanitize(obj) recursive helper inside the ar_reconcile endpoint.
  - Handles all pandas/numpy non-JSON-safe types:
      pd.Timestamp  → ISO 8601 string, or None if NaT
      pd.NaT        → None
      float NaN     → None
      np.integer    → int
      np.floating   → float, or None if NaN
      np.bool_      → bool
      dict/list     → recursed into
  - Applied _sanitize() to the entire response dict before jsonify(),
    covering all paths: results, tier2_rows, src_report, tgt_report.
  - Also applied to the 422 error response (not_found mapping report).
  - IMPACT: Any pandas/numpy value anywhere in the AR reconcile response
    is now safely converted before serialization. No more 500 regardless
    of which columns contain NaT or NaN.


